#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"}] :question/id #uuid "36127b89-97ba-5f98-bd8e-b36ae1a261f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-896" :question/problem "The gravitational acceleration $g$ of a body in free fall is given by the formula\n\n$$\ng = \\dfrac{2h}{t^2},\n$$\n\n where $h$ is the distance, measured in meters, traveled by the body due to gravity in time $t,$ measured in seconds.\nDetermine an appropriate measurement unit for gravitational acceleration." :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of gravitational acceleration, we substitute the given units of distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g & =\\frac{2h}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}.$ Here, we ignore the unitless constant $2$ since it does not affect the units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e1b4e78-8b4a-59ea-8fac-9092c2eeb67a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is Cancellation"}] :question/id #uuid "ee3fa65e-1146-598a-a89d-33d287e88066" :question/math-academy-id "e-897" :question/problem "The frequency of a wave is given by the formula\n\n$$\nf = \\dfrac{v}{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the wave in $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}},$ while $l$ represents the length of the wave in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for frequency." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of frequency by substituting the given units of velocity and length into the formula and simplifying. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f & =\\frac{v}{l} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{m}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of frequency is $\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{s}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "deb16829-8ec8-5cbb-b81f-2c526bce165e" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Nonstandard Units"}] :question/id #uuid "b0212d4c-eb7a-5cec-8eb1-1c60be355f4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-898" :question/problem "A farm with $115$ chickens produces $20\\,000$ eggs per year.  To determine the number of eggs $P$ produced by one chicken on average, the farmer uses the formula\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{N}{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of eggs produced by the farm during the year, and $C$ is the number of chickens on the farm. \n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $115$ and $20\\,000.$ They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $N$ represents $\\textrm{eggs}$ and $C$ represents $\\textrm{chickens},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $P=\\dfrac{N}{C}$ is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{eggs}}{\\textrm{chickens}}.$ In words, this is \"eggs per chicken.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e1b4e78-8b4a-59ea-8fac-9092c2eeb67a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is Cancellation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232f8a61-dc2b-5713-b945-81e4ad13440b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495588} {:answer/id #uuid "23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495589} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9cd203-1c3a-51d2-9094-86b40b32f3d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495689} {:answer/id #uuid "dc62581e-c5c7-5bf5-993f-988441a4c219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}\\,\\text{kg}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495995} {:answer/id #uuid "e5473d53-bc7d-58b4-97c6-6181108fc8a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23962e40-074b-56c5-9c89-0e1447aaad44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495589} :answer-field/id #uuid "1658fceb-3d29-5e01-ae74-ca2195773a25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eced41ae-3fab-5c67-a3bf-aedcd9f64978" :question/math-academy-id "q-105382" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two particles of mass ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ that are at a distance $r$ from each other is given by the formula ${F}_{\\text{G}}=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}},$ where $G$ is the gravitational constant measured in $\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}},$ masses ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are measured in kilograms, and the distance $r$ is measured in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for the gravitational force." :question/worked-solution "We can find the unit of gravitational force by substituting the given unit of the gravitational constant, mass, and distance into the formula and simplifying it.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{\\text{G}} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{kg}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\cdot \\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\,\\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the gravitational force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e1b4e78-8b4a-59ea-8fac-9092c2eeb67a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is Cancellation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fce19de-60b6-585b-a388-1fa1eeb5a3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495694} {:answer/id #uuid "79a01367-cc89-5f4a-869f-97de82fd3129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{m}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495771} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0d7771-34e6-5d10-a9a3-70e0207f524d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495782} {:answer/id #uuid "894020f7-35dc-5ea3-907a-86bdf3be687c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{m}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495809} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ce8dc-668f-526f-89a8-3f0762530def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495895} :answer-field/id #uuid "f644e2e4-a4d8-546c-9e9b-b5558284b434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d7c30aa-a3e9-5b45-be5b-cfccc139072e" :question/math-academy-id "q-105397" :question/problem "The magnitude of a force $F$ applied to an object is related to the work $W$ and the distance $d$ by the formula $F=\\frac{W}{d}.$  Given that $W$ is measured in $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and the distance is measured in meters, determine an appropriate measurement unit for the force." :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "deb16829-8ec8-5cbb-b81f-2c526bce165e" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Nonstandard Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "465d914d-5e5f-5208-9d20-21251f1b74ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{cars}\\cdot {\\text{seller}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495669} {:answer/id #uuid "75d4f1b5-f48c-5114-bb9b-77b789e0e67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{cars}\\cdot \\text{seller}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495767} {:answer/id #uuid "adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495898} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a71fc2-2e3f-5004-8f69-4b70fb5b946a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{sellers}}{\\text{car}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495915} {:answer/id #uuid "eca4b609-64f1-5eff-a923-cb5c2dedb208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cars}}{{\\text{seller}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb28f05-385d-5ece-aea8-14d47ee20d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495898} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6021314-f875-5954-b86b-11e994619d15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "177c4027-65c0-5159-869d-c7c5edbf2c0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105441" :question/problem "A car dealership with $7$ sellers sells $50$ cars in a month. The owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{c}{s}\n$$\n\nto determine, on average, the number of cars sold by each seller, where $c$ is the total of cars sold, and $s$ is the total number of sellers.\n\nFind an appropriate measurement unit for $N.$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $50$ and $7.$ They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $c$ represents $\\text{cars}$ and $s$ represents $\\text{sellers},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{c}{s}$ is $\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14976fe2-9b52-5ebe-8c73-8ff28cb9c14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{s}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{kg}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495554} {:answer/id #uuid "7c54496e-2150-55be-ad11-f0813a6820d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495776} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495920} {:answer/id #uuid "c72b361b-647a-5fbe-98d9-e79fe7657b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495945} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4b5f65-0be1-558d-93d2-208a32c43b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d75902-8118-5803-a45a-9a41127624e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495920} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c9253cc-52ac-5d1c-b3ee-ba5595fbaccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f927f6ff-ccc6-56f9-848c-de38a72fd309" :question/math-academy-id "q-46081" :question/problem "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of work, we substitute the given units of force and distance into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =F\\cdot d \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "deb16829-8ec8-5cbb-b81f-2c526bce165e" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Nonstandard Units"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Letters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495581} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6e619d-74a8-5230-bbd2-35edd9e4cf43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Letters per cubic minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495603} {:answer/id #uuid "937a97e5-808c-5a30-8887-12355f82c9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Minutes per letter" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495835} {:answer/id #uuid "c009fba3-f728-5ed7-aafe-3f93f120520d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Cubic letters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495932} {:answer/id #uuid "cd23376a-66eb-56d2-acfe-654ce73059e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Letters per squared minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21377026-996d-57b0-b029-a4d76cc8f596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Letters per minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495581} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a81c31-3d9d-5c47-8101-840c572bf6c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff3d9c94-77b4-51bb-806f-e93681c391b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-46090" :question/problem "A printer prints $1\\,250$ letters in $60$ minutes. To determine the number of letters that the printer prints on average, the owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{l}{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the number of letters printed and $t$ the time in minutes.\n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $N?$" :question/worked-solution "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $1\\,250$ and $60.$ They do not matter, if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $l$ represents $\\text{letters}$ and $t$ represents $\\text{minute},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{l}{t}$ is $\\frac{\\text{letters}}{\\text{minute}}.$ In words, this is 'letters per minute'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495520} {:answer/id #uuid "1de930e2-59b8-55c2-9276-ff6f790b54aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495574} {:answer/id #uuid "40256bc8-0f04-5875-90c7-812b6705e3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495659} {:answer/id #uuid "6f34b9f2-d299-5888-b1a9-3477433b7d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{m}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{kg}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495754} {:answer/id #uuid "927f33a2-7b1f-56c9-8fca-fb0a267ad22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "037cca16-f4b6-599c-b43a-a42f90a74451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495520} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b5e27f-972a-5f60-9f56-8e1482b67a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4eb324e-31ac-540b-94ba-f7c7a69f257d" :question/math-academy-id "q-46150" :question/problem "The formula that relates the work $W$ applied to an object with mass $m$, distance $d$ and time $t$ is $W=\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}.$ What are the units of work in terms of base units?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit of work, we substitute the base units of mass, distance, and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d762ea4-afec-5d24-987b-1629fe8078d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\,{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495571} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfc7a0e-24fa-511e-8e2a-c033f6a57641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495686} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495691} {:answer/id #uuid "daaa803f-6528-5c7c-ab17-6235345378e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495993} {:answer/id #uuid "defedfc3-6a0c-570a-8e21-65d713653ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ed65f3f-f541-5dbc-b19e-e2762e996517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495691} :answer-field/id #uuid "50db8c22-bd5b-5673-ad8f-e5d45d8e0739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e4a27b0-0664-5456-aded-ea139acd3d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-46155" :question/problem "The work applied to an object is the result of multiplying the force by the distance. Given that the force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}),$ determine the unit of work in terms of kilograms, meters and seconds." :question/worked-solution "The force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and distance is measured in meters ($\\text{m}$). Since the work is the multiplication of force by distance, we must multiply force units by distance units. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$"}]}
